Apologize? For what?

252
00:13:52,920 --> 00:13:54,280
He was the one that lied to us.

253
00:13:54,320 --> 00:13:55,900
Well, I shouldn't have lost my temper, Maggie.

254
00:13:55,940 --> 00:13:58,060
If ever there was a time for reason, that was it.

255
00:13:58,340 --> 00:14:01,140
Jason, you are not going to apologize to anyone.

256
00:14:01,740 --> 00:14:03,160
This is a time to get tough.

257
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:04,960
This is a time to forget reasoning.

258
00:14:05,020 --> 00:14:06,600
Remember, we're the parents.

259
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:08,060
We're in charge.

260
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:11,160
Let's just tell him that if he doesn't go to school, the freebies stop.

261
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:14,400
You're right. I'm going to bed.

262
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:19,060
Good. Honey, the bedroom's this way.

263
00:14:20,740 --> 00:14:22,760
We're gonna get a bite to eat.

264
00:15:11,660 --> 00:15:13,960
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

265
00:15:13,960 --> 00:15:14,180
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

266
00:15:15,740 --> 00:15:17,300
Okay, go ahead.

267
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:20,040
I like to do it face to face, Mike.

268
00:15:27,700 --> 00:15:28,700
Mike, open this door.

269
00:15:28,780 --> 00:15:29,520
I'll rip it off.

270
00:15:32,940 --> 00:15:36,340
Dad, you know, I've been going through the Boynton catalog here, and if I pick up

271
00:15:36,340 --> 00:15:39,660
some weekend courses, I can probably get my teaching degree in about 12 years.

272
00:15:41,240 --> 00:15:43,380
Mike, you don't have to put on any act with me, okay?

273
00:15:43,380 --> 00:15:46,540
I just, I wanted to finish the conversation that we should have finished earlier

274
00:15:46,540 --> 00:15:47,620
before I lost my temper.

275
00:15:47,820 --> 00:15:50,660
I just want to get to the heart of this, the meat, the very marrow of the matter.

276
00:15:51,900 --> 00:15:53,040
You're gonna hit me, aren't you?

277
00:15:55,020 --> 00:15:57,180
Come on, come on, where's that list you had yesterday?

278
00:15:57,180 --> 00:16:00,460
I want to go over some of your reasons for why you should stay in school.

279
00:16:01,780 --> 00:16:03,400
Uh, I lost it.

280
00:16:05,340 --> 00:16:06,480
It's right here.

281
00:16:09,140 --> 00:16:11,000
This is a Xerox of my list.

282
00:16:13,380 --> 00:16:15,840
Mike, you rooted through your mother's underwear drawer.

283
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:19,120
Yeah, well, you've been in my sock drawer.

284
00:16:19,280 --> 00:16:20,960
Well, it's hardly the same thing.

285
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:22,080
I'll tell that to Ben.

286
00:16:22,440 --> 00:16:25,680
Can we just stick to the reason I came in here?

287
00:16:25,760 --> 00:16:26,800
Okay, all right.

288
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:28,420
Right, you were gonna come to apologize.

289
00:16:29,900 --> 00:16:34,680
Mike, what you did, you lied to me.

290
00:16:36,520 --> 00:16:37,880
Apology accepted. Good night.

291
00:16:38,420 --> 00:16:40,240
Mike, you don't understand something.

292
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:42,180
I am not trying to make you suffer.

293
00:16:42,780 --> 00:16:44,380
Well, Dad, you gotta understand something.

294
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:46,120
I've got some things that I want to do.

295
00:16:46,220 --> 00:16:47,560
Okay, answer me this, Mike.

296
00:16:47,760 --> 00:16:52,360
Do you really think it's a good idea to go off and pursue your acting bug without a

297
00:16:52,360 --> 00:16:53,480
college degree? Is that practical?

298
00:16:54,580 --> 00:16:55,220
No, it isn't.

299
00:16:55,380 --> 00:16:56,440
Okay, we're getting somewhere.

300
00:16:56,460 --> 00:16:57,940
I don't need to be practical, Dad.

301
00:16:57,980 --> 00:16:59,980
I've got talent, okay?

302
00:17:00,380 --> 00:17:01,920
And besides, it is not a bug.

303
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:03,780
I mean, that's part of the problem I think we have here.

304
00:17:03,880 --> 00:17:06,380
I mean, when I'm interested in doing something, it's a bug.

305
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:09,320
And when Carol's interested, you call it an aspiration.

306
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:13,880
You leave your sister's aspiration out of it.

307
00:17:14,580 --> 00:17:16,240
Now, I'm just trying to be reasonable, Mike.

308
00:17:16,240 --> 00:17:17,080
No, you're not, Dad.

309
00:17:17,340 --> 00:17:20,600
You're just trying to talk me into doing what you want me to do because you want me

310
00:17:20,600 --> 00:17:21,080
to do it.

311
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:22,980
But, Dad, I am 20 years old, okay?

312
00:17:23,400 --> 00:17:29,500
I am old enough to vote, to die for my country, and if I so desire, to have a beer

313
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:30,320
in Puerto Rico.

314
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:34,800
Oh, I can see how serious you are about this acting thing.

315
00:17:35,140 --> 00:17:36,960
Oh. Oh, versus the bug, now it's a thing?

316
00:17:37,140 --> 00:17:38,940
Come on, Dad, why don't you just call it what it is?

317
00:17:38,940 --> 00:17:40,220
Okay, Mike, you know what it is?

318
00:17:40,260 --> 00:17:41,600
It's a sickness, Mike!

319
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:44,700
I mean, it's one job you got for 250 bucks.

320
00:17:44,740 --> 00:17:46,540
What is it, for a week, a month, a year?

321
00:17:46,560 --> 00:17:47,340
Who knows? And then what?

322
00:17:47,340 --> 00:17:49,260
You come running, you're high...

323
00:17:49,260 --> 00:17:52,160
Ah. We're far too emotional here.

324
00:17:53,660 --> 00:17:57,520
How is the guy supposed to respond when his dad squishes his dream like a bug?

325
00:17:57,780 --> 00:18:00,020
Oh! So we admit it's a bug.

326
00:18:00,380 --> 00:18:05,920
You know? Okay, you know, it's probably a bad idea to discuss this right now.

327
00:18:05,980 --> 00:18:08,380
Let's just wait, and we'll do it in the morning, okay?

328
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:15,020
Fine. We can talk about it in the morning, Dad, but things are not gonna change.

329
00:18:15,260 --> 00:18:17,700
Oh, Mike, you're making this real tough on me.

330
00:18:17,700 --> 00:18:19,520
Dad, look, it's no picnic for me either.

331
00:18:19,980 --> 00:18:21,800
I have got a rehearsal tomorrow at nine.

332
00:18:21,980 --> 00:18:24,060
Well, maybe you just won't go to that rehearsal, Mike.

333
00:18:24,180 --> 00:18:27,200
I mean, maybe there's a middle ground for us.

334
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:29,020
I mean, we've been through a lot of stuff together.

335
00:18:29,140 --> 00:18:30,800
Why can't we just find a compromise?

336
00:18:31,900 --> 00:18:33,380
Well, I would sure like to.

337
00:18:33,540 --> 00:18:34,200
Okay, all right.

338
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:35,360
What sounds fair to you?

339
00:18:35,780 --> 00:18:40,240
Okay. I think that I should quit school and take the acting job.

340
00:18:41,480 --> 00:18:42,700
How's that a compromise?

341
00:18:43,780 --> 00:18:45,320
You can still yell at me.

342
00:18:46,600 --> 00:18:47,860
I'll tell you what a compromise is.

343
00:18:48,020 --> 00:18:51,380
A compromise is you go to school and you do your plays on the weekend.

344
00:18:51,500 --> 00:18:52,860
Dad, plays don't work that way.

345
00:18:52,920 --> 00:18:54,200
Well, they can for a couple years.

346
00:18:54,240 --> 00:18:56,280
Look, Dad, there is going to be no compromise, okay?

347
00:18:56,280 --> 00:18:59,960
I am going to do what I want to do, and you are just going to have to live with

348
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:03,140
that. Okay, Mike, here's the deal.

349
00:19:04,040 --> 00:19:05,200
You want that same rent?

350
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:06,700
You want the Seaver Meal plan?

351
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:07,860
Then you gotta study something!

352
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:08,900
You gotta be a student!

353
00:19:09,180 --> 00:19:10,620
Study something anywhere, I don't care.

354
00:19:11,120 --> 00:19:14,240
I hear in California now they got a school for game show hosts.

355
00:19:14,660 --> 00:19:15,340
Ha ha ha!

356
00:19:17,260 --> 00:19:19,040
What, Alex Trebek tech?

357
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:25,660
I'm serious. So you're saying if I don't do what you say, you're making me move out?

358
00:19:25,740 --> 00:19:27,740
No, Mike, you're choosing to move out.

359
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:30,420
But why? I don't know, you tell me.

360
00:19:30,440 --> 00:19:31,840
I like you living here.

361
00:19:32,360 --> 00:19:33,480
Well, me too, Dad.

362
00:19:33,480 --> 00:19:35,640
So what are we fighting about?

363
00:19:36,660 --> 00:19:37,980
We're not fighting anymore.

364
00:19:39,520 --> 00:19:41,460
I want your decision in the morning.

365
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:05,180
You're up early.

366
00:20:06,380 --> 00:20:07,580
We got doors to hang.

367
00:20:09,520 --> 00:20:12,960
Doors? Plural? You talked to Mike after you said you wouldn't.

368
00:20:13,740 --> 00:20:15,660
I happened to run into him last night.

369
00:20:16,460 --> 00:20:18,820
Was this about the time you were rattling dishes in the kitchen?

370
00:20:20,300 --> 00:20:23,680
Later. And? And what?

371
00:20:24,260 --> 00:20:26,380
And he owns the house and you're his handyman?

372
00:20:28,340 --> 00:20:32,640
The reason I'm leaving this early is so hopefully I can make it into Manhattan under

373
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:34,480
two hours if anybody cares.

374
00:20:35,380 --> 00:20:36,960
Have a good day, sweetheart.

375
00:20:37,280 --> 00:20:40,740
Ditto. Mom, may I borrow your mace?

376
00:20:40,860 --> 00:20:42,360
I used all mine up yesterday.

377
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:46,780
Sure, honey. Jason, what happened?

378
00:20:47,580 --> 00:20:51,260
Well, just had a little talk, and I think I got through to him.

379
00:20:51,680 --> 00:20:52,640
You do? Really?

380
00:20:52,800 --> 00:20:53,860
Mm -hmm. Really.

381
00:20:54,260 --> 00:20:56,840
Well, maybe it's a good thing you did talk to him.

382
00:20:57,140 --> 00:21:01,360
If I'd gone up there, I probably would have lost my temper and given him some stupid

383
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:08,660
ultimatum. I'm gonna go get the newspaper.

384
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:19,100
Hey, Mike. Can I hang that door for you?

385
00:21:19,940 --> 00:21:52,020
Mike? I don't care what they say to me.

386
00:21:52,020 --> 00:21:56,700
Only the echoes of mine are mine.

387
00:21:59,620 --> 00:22:01,780
People start to stare.

388
00:22:02,640 --> 00:22:03,220
I'm just a little girl.

389
00:22:03,220 --> 00:22:11,360
I can see their faces, only the shadows of their eyes.

390
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:20,920
I'm going where the sun keeps shining, through the pouring rain.

391
00:22:26,560 --> 00:22:28,880
What are we going to do about Mike?

392
00:22:29,040 --> 00:22:31,000
We can't just let him live here and be a bum.

393
00:22:31,160 --> 00:22:32,320
I don't know what to do.

394
00:22:32,320 --> 00:22:34,500
Our only hope is that he comes to his senses.

395
00:22:34,660 --> 00:22:35,900
Then we have no hope.

396
00:22:37,300 --> 00:22:38,180
Kate, I did it.

397
00:22:38,180 --> 00:22:39,960
I moved to New York and I'm starring in a play.

398
00:22:40,220 --> 00:22:42,020
Broadway? I'm off Broadway.

399
00:22:42,260 --> 00:22:42,860
How far off?

400
00:22:43,260 --> 00:22:44,740
There's a nursing home on the second floor.

401
00:22:45,700 --> 00:22:49,140
Doesn't matter. The point is here I am and here you are and heck, have you seen

402
00:22:49,140 --> 00:22:50,520
anyone? No. Well, me neither.

403
00:22:50,820 --> 00:22:51,360
Wanna live together?

404
00:23:01,840 --> 00:23:02,200
["The New York Times"]
